WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.014
Phrasal Verbs and Idioms.

2
00:00:02.294 --> 00:00:05.985
Day 13 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the English phase.

3
00:00:07.735 --> 00:00:08.759
The key idea.

4
00:00:09.039 --> 00:00:22.799
Today's goal: understand and use the phrasal verbs and idioms that native speakers and global colleagues use all the time (follow up, sort out, on the same page) so you understand meetings better and sound more natural.

5
00:00:24.149 --> 00:00:25.002
Part 1.

6
00:00:25.282 --> 00:00:26.605
What is a phrasal verb?

7
00:00:27.855 --> 00:00:34.703
look = see, look into = investigate, look forward to = be excited about, look up = search for.

8
00:00:36.053 --> 00:00:36.927
Part 2.

9
00:00:37.207 --> 00:00:39.661
30 phrasal verbs for work and daily life.

10
00:00:40.911 --> 00:00:42.255
Here is a quick table:

11
00:00:42.535 --> 00:00:45.863
Phrasal verb, and Meaning, and Example. follow up (on).

12
00:00:46.143 --> 00:00:47.764
Meaning: check again later.

13
00:00:48.044 --> 00:00:48.919
Example:

14
00:00:49.199 --> 00:00:51.972
I'll follow up with the vendor tomorrow. look into.

15
00:00:52.252 --> 00:00:53.703
Meaning: investigate.

16
00:00:53.983 --> 00:00:54.857
Example:

17
00:00:55.137 --> 00:00:57.527
Could you look into the login bug? sort out.

18
00:00:57.807 --> 00:00:59.215
Meaning: fix, solve.

19
00:00:59.495 --> 00:01:00.369
Example:

20
00:01:00.649 --> 00:01:03.337
We need to sort out the access issue. figure out.

21
00:01:03.617 --> 00:01:05.900
Meaning: understand, find the answer.

22
00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:07.055
Example:

23
00:01:07.335 --> 00:01:10.300
I can't figure out why the test fails. come up with.

24
00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:12.265
Meaning: think of (an idea).

25
00:01:12.545 --> 00:01:13.420
Example:

26
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:16.089
She came up with a better design. bring up.

27
00:01:16.369 --> 00:01:18.055
Meaning: mention a topic.

28
00:01:18.335 --> 00:01:19.209
Example:

29
00:01:19.489 --> 00:01:21.793
Can I bring up one more point? point out.

30
00:01:22.073 --> 00:01:23.780
Meaning: draw attention to.

31
00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:24.935
Example:

32
00:01:25.215 --> 00:01:27.604
He pointed out a security risk. set up.

33
00:01:27.884 --> 00:01:29.825
Meaning: arrange, configure.

34
00:01:30.105 --> 00:01:30.980
Example:

35
00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.076
Let's set up a call. / I set up the server.

36
00:01:35.426 --> 00:01:36.322
carry out.

37
00:01:36.602 --> 00:01:38.053
Meaning: do, perform.

38
00:01:38.333 --> 00:01:39.207
Example:

39
00:01:39.487 --> 00:01:43.178
We carried out load tests last week. roll out or roll back.

40
00:01:43.458 --> 00:01:46.061
Meaning: release gradually or undo a release.

41
00:01:46.341 --> 00:01:47.215
Example:

42
00:01:47.495 --> 00:01:49.373
We'll roll out to 10% first.

43
00:01:49.653 --> 00:01:51.701
We rolled back the update. put off.

44
00:01:51.981 --> 00:01:53.346
Meaning: postpone.

45
00:01:53.626 --> 00:01:54.501
Example:

46
00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:57.511
Let's put off the meeting until Monday. call off.

47
00:01:57.791 --> 00:01:59.071
Meaning: cancel.

48
00:01:59.351 --> 00:02:00.226
Example:

49
00:02:00.506 --> 00:02:02.874
They called off the offsite. catch up (with).

50
00:02:03.154 --> 00:02:04.946
Meaning: talk to get updated.

51
00:02:05.226 --> 00:02:06.101
Example:

52
00:02:06.381 --> 00:02:08.813
Let's catch up after lunch. fill in (for).

53
00:02:09.093 --> 00:02:11.226
Meaning: replace someone temporarily.

54
00:02:11.506 --> 00:02:12.381
Example:

55
00:02:12.661 --> 00:02:15.754
Can you fill in for me at stand-up? fill out or fill in.

56
00:02:16.034 --> 00:02:17.570
Meaning: complete a form.

57
00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:18.725
Example:

58
00:02:19.005 --> 00:02:21.309
Please fill out this form. get back to.

59
00:02:21.589 --> 00:02:23.082
Meaning: reply later.

60
00:02:23.362 --> 00:02:24.237
Example:

61
00:02:24.517 --> 00:02:26.181
I'll get back to you by Friday.

62
00:02:27.531 --> 00:02:28.469
hand over.

63
00:02:28.749 --> 00:02:30.755
Meaning: give responsibility to.

64
00:02:31.035 --> 00:02:31.909
Example:

65
00:02:32.189 --> 00:02:35.155
I'll hand over my tasks before leave. take over.

66
00:02:35.435 --> 00:02:37.440
Meaning: take responsibility from.

67
00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:38.595
Example:

68
00:02:38.875 --> 00:02:41.499
Ravi will take over the project. run into.

69
00:02:41.779 --> 00:02:44.445
Meaning: meet a problem or person by chance.

70
00:02:44.725 --> 00:02:45.600
Example:

71
00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:48.440
We ran into a memory issue. run out of.

72
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.107
Meaning: have no more.

73
00:02:50.387 --> 00:02:51.261
Example:

74
00:02:51.541 --> 00:02:53.333
We've run out of time. back up.

75
00:02:53.613 --> 00:02:55.960
Meaning: copy data or support someone.

76
00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:57.115
Example:

77
00:02:57.395 --> 00:02:58.739
Back up the database.

78
00:02:59.019 --> 00:03:01.216
My manager backed me up. go over.

79
00:03:01.496 --> 00:03:02.755
Meaning: review.

80
00:03:03.035 --> 00:03:03.909
Example:

81
00:03:04.189 --> 00:03:06.685
Let's go over the plan once more. work out.

82
00:03:06.965 --> 00:03:08.800
Meaning: solve; exercise.

83
00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:09.955
Example:

84
00:03:10.235 --> 00:03:13.563
It'll work out. / I work out at the gym. turn down.

85
00:03:13.843 --> 00:03:15.997
Meaning: refuse; lower volume.

86
00:03:16.277 --> 00:03:17.152
Example:

87
00:03:17.432 --> 00:03:18.819
She turned down the offer.

88
00:03:20.169 --> 00:03:20.979
show up.

89
00:03:21.259 --> 00:03:22.561
Meaning: arrive.

90
00:03:22.841 --> 00:03:23.715
Example:

91
00:03:23.995 --> 00:03:26.555
Only five people showed up. drop by.

92
00:03:26.835 --> 00:03:28.435
Meaning: visit briefly.

93
00:03:28.715 --> 00:03:29.590
Example:

94
00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:32.643
I'll drop by your desk. log in or log out.

95
00:03:32.923 --> 00:03:34.715
Meaning: sign in or sign out.

96
00:03:34.995 --> 00:03:35.870
Example:

97
00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:37.793
I can't log in. pick up.

98
00:03:38.073 --> 00:03:39.907
Meaning: collect; learn quickly.

99
00:03:40.187 --> 00:03:41.062
Example:

100
00:03:41.342 --> 00:03:42.665
I'll pick you up at 8.

101
00:03:42.945 --> 00:03:45.910
She picked up Kotlin fast. check in or check out.

102
00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:48.899
Meaning: arrive and register or leave (hotel).

103
00:03:49.179 --> 00:03:50.054
Example:

104
00:03:50.334 --> 00:03:52.361
We checked in at noon. find out.

105
00:03:52.641 --> 00:03:53.985
Meaning: discover.

106
00:03:54.265 --> 00:03:55.139
Example:

107
00:03:55.419 --> 00:03:57.169
I'll find out and let you know.

108
00:03:58.519 --> 00:03:59.415
Part 3.

109
00:03:59.695 --> 00:04:01.444
20 idioms you'll hear at work.

110
00:04:02.694 --> 00:04:04.038
Here is a quick table:

111
00:04:04.318 --> 00:04:23.177
Idiom, and Meaning. on the same page: agreeing or understanding the same thing. touch base: have a short conversation to update. ballpark figure: rough estimate. low-hanging fruit: easy wins. the ball is in your court: it's your turn to decide or act. back to square one: start again from the

112
00:04:23.457 --> 00:04:29.771
beginning. a heads-up: early warning. in the loop or keep me posted: informed or keep me updated.

113
00:04:31.121 --> 00:04:52.092
hit the ground running: start working fast and well. cut corners: do it badly to save time. get the ball rolling: start something. think outside the box: think creatively. on the back burner: low priority for now. move the needle: make a real difference. bite the bullet: do something hard you've been avoiding. a win-win: good for both sides.

114
00:04:53.442 --> 00:05:07.202
take it offline: discuss later, separately. bandwidth: time or capacity (I don't have bandwidth this week). a quick win: a small success that comes fast. crunch time: the busy, stressful period before a deadline.

115
00:05:08.552 --> 00:05:09.192
Tip:

116
00:05:09.472 --> 00:05:11.307
Understand many, use a few.

117
00:05:11.587 --> 00:05:14.019
You don't need to use idioms to sound professional.

118
00:05:14.299 --> 00:05:18.587
Understand them when others use them, and use only the ones you're comfortable with.

119
00:05:18.867 --> 00:05:22.109
With mixed international audiences, plain English is clearer.

120
00:05:23.459 --> 00:05:24.377
Part 4.

121
00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:26.385
Conversation: a quick sync.

122
00:05:27.635 --> 00:05:29.661
Here is how the conversation goes.

123
00:05:29.941 --> 00:05:33.248
Quick heads-up: the client wants to roll out the new app next Monday.

124
00:05:33.528 --> 00:05:35.064
That's earlier than planned.

125
00:05:35.344 --> 00:05:37.819
Are we all on the same page about the scope?

126
00:05:38.099 --> 00:05:39.315
Mostly.

127
00:05:39.595 --> 00:05:42.453
Can you look into the payment issue Meena brought up yesterday?

128
00:05:42.733 --> 00:05:43.459
Sure.

129
00:05:43.739 --> 00:05:45.893
I ran into it last week too.

130
00:05:46.173 --> 00:05:48.755
I think I can sort it out by Wednesday.

131
00:05:50.105 --> 00:05:51.705
The conversation continues.

132
00:05:51.985 --> 00:05:52.923
Great.

133
00:05:53.203 --> 00:05:56.937
If it takes longer, give me a heads-up so I can keep the client in the loop.

134
00:05:57.217 --> 00:05:58.177
Will do.

135
00:05:58.457 --> 00:06:00.569
Should we put off the analytics feature?

136
00:06:00.849 --> 00:06:03.195
Yes, let's put it on the back burner for now.

137
00:06:03.475 --> 00:06:04.414
Okay.

138
00:06:04.694 --> 00:06:08.470
I'll follow up with Meena and get back to you this afternoon.

139
00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:10.780
Part 5.

140
